Z||000000||Homicidal||06-29-2001||08:18 PM||||I was driving home, nothing abnormal going on with the car.  I had to quickly hit my brakes due to a slow driver cutting me off, and then when I re-applied the accelerator, for some reason my speakers fizzled out, and I heard just my sub going, the headunit was still on.  Then the headunit blinked off and everything shut off (radio wise)!  Then I smelled a heavy electronic burning coming from the headunit/console area!!<P>Now the headunit won't turn back on.  Fuses on the amp and my inline fuse are all good.  I can only think that the jolt of electricity from the quick flooring of the car overloaded the headunit.<P>Any other ideas?  How can I test it further?<P>------------------<BR><B>It's sad, in the 20th Century, in the 1990's, we would have to still go the same bullshit route<BR>that artists have to go, to get acceptance.  If it wasn't for the independence, if it wasn't for the <BR>small little cities, and the few little ghetto guys, trying to make music, it never would have happened.<BR>Some of these guys will never make a dime, some of thse guys will be poor and die, alone.  <P>But in the proccess, they've been the true renegades.<P>And the true rebels always walk alone anyways.</B>||||reg||8
Z||000001||DecadenceR||06-29-2001||08:35 PM||||Pull out the head unit. If it's an aftermarket, check the fuse they have in the back of the unit. Make sure all your connections are good. Sounds like a loose wire could have caused a short.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-29-2001||08:43 PM||||go over the wiring etc... sound like soemthing moved and pinched something. May have been loose trash in the unit that decided to move and shorted something inside it.||||reg||1
